From 7592e7283853bfcf3ac2acb787843bea9849d974 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AuraLite Patch <noreply@auralite.local>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 15:07:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] FSMODERN M0: the benchmark, and the first honest numbers

Not one of the tree's thirteen filesystems had a single number attached to
it, which made "make our own filesystem very fast" unfalsifiable. This
phase ships the instrument and the first table.

- tests/unit/test_fsbench.c: the host lane. Links the SHIPPED
  buffer_cache.c, blkdev.c and diskfs.c (not copies) against a RAM-backed
  blkdev_ops that counts every sector, and drives the real vfs_ops table.
  It measures what a RAM device can honestly measure: correctness, write
  amplification (1.016x for diskfs) and device call counts. Every MiB/s it
  prints is RAM-to-RAM and the tool says so in its own output.
- tools/fsbench/fsbench.c -> /bin/fsbench: the in-guest lane, where the
  numbers are real. create -> write -> sync() -> read back and VERIFY ->
  unlink, one stable [fsbench] label=value line per metric.
- tests/integration/cases/test_fsbench.sh (fs shard, 13/13): drives both
  lanes with test_diskfs.sh's AHCI recipe so /disk is really mounted, and
  pins shape, not speed.
- docs/filesystems_perf.md: the table, with the date and the hardware.
  tmpfs 16.98 MiB/s write / 36.55 MiB/s read; diskfs 1.15 MiB/s write /
  5.12 MiB/s read, sync 14930 us for 16 KiB.

Three findings from building the instrument:

1. bc_evict() does not evict. It frees a buffer by setting block_num = -1
   but leaves it linked in the LRU, so a loop over it frees the same buffer
   forever (measured: 4096 calls, then a full re-read gave 204 hits / 0
   misses). A real block-layer defect; recorded for M2.
2. diskfs has no cold path. Its whole volume is 128 KiB and the cache is
   512 KiB, so the filesystem is permanently cache-resident. The host lane
   asserts reread_misses == 0 instead of inventing a throughput.
3. The first in-guest run reported 31250 MiB/s, and it was a lie.
   clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC) here is PIT-driven at 99 Hz; a 32 KiB run
   finishes inside one 10.1 ms tick, so every delta was zero and sync=0 us.
   Fixed by timing with RDTSC (CR4.TSD is never set, so ring 3 may read the
   TSC), calibrated against the tick clock -- and the receipt now prints
   clock=tsc so a reader never has to guess. The integration case asserts
   that string.

Gates: make test-unit 260/260 exit 0; test_fsbench 13/13; registry 241
cases, one shard each.
