+From 63dad791d1f62aa38020e2fc8e7da96dc395c827 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: AuraLite Patch <noreply@auralite.local>
+Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 14:27:34 +0000
+Subject: [PATCH 2/2] FSMODERN: the filesystem modernization plan (M0-M7)
+
+The user's brief: make our own filesystem very fast and make it the OS's
+flagship. This plan states up front what makes that hard today -- not one of
+the tree's thirteen filesystems has a number attached to it, so "fast" is
+currently unfalsifiable.
+
+M0   measure: an in-guest benchmark receipt + a host harness in make
+     test-unit + docs/filesystems_perf.md generated by the gate
+M1   a filesystem on the boot disk by default (/data), with a
+     write-reboot-read-back test -- the first durability proof in the tree
+M2   the block layer: 4 KiB cache blocks, readahead, write coalescing
+     (the cache is 512 KiB today: 512 B x 1024)
+M3   the durability contract: fs_sync(vnode, mode), a power-cut test, and
+     an explicit FS_DURABILITY_WEAK declaration instead of folklore
+M4   ext2/fat32 completeness (mount-time bitmap check; FAT-before-data
+     ordering on fsync)
+M5   hashed directories, in the flagship only
+M6   AuraFS: log-structured allocation evolved from btrfs.c (CoW + SHA-256
+     already right), with a bounded, measured speed claim
+M7   publish the number
+
+D2 records why this is not a tenth filesystem; HOME_PLAN H4 is deliberately
+gated behind M1+M3.
